  "split_filter": {
    "eval_split": null,
    "slices": [],
    "rows_removed": {},
    "rule": "No split filter: the kpis, exceedance and map slices hold the rows of every evaluation split the runs stored (their eval_split column)."
  },
  "rules": {
    "infinity": "Strict JSON has no infinities: an infinite value is written as null, in JSON and in Arrow alike, only in the columns whose manifest entry has null_means '-inf' (payouts.lower: the zero row of a below_attachment 'zero' run, param_index -1, covers the index range below the attachment). Any other infinite value is refused at export.",
    "nan": "NaN is never written. In a column whose store schema allows undefined values it is written as null, and the column's manifest entry gives its null count and what null means there; in any other column it is refused at export. A column has at most one meaning of null.",
    "types": "Integers are 32-bit (Arrow JS reads 64-bit integers as BigInt); strings are dictionary-encoded UTF-8 with the narrowest signed index (int8, int16 or int32); floats are 64-bit.",
    "arrow": "Tables are Arrow IPC files (.arrow), uncompressed, one record batch: Quarto OJS reads them with FileAttachment(path).arrow().",
    "budget": "Every file, the manifest included, fits max_file_bytes, and the data files together fit max_bundle_bytes (both in bytes). Over budget is an error at export, never a truncation.",
    "checksums": "Every data file is listed under files with its size in bytes and the SHA-256 of its bytes; the manifest itself is not."
  },
  "map": {
    "rule": "Per run, evaluation and location: the location's expected loss, payout and uncovered loss. They come from the run's per-location KPI rows under an allocation rule, which splits each design problem's payout across its locations (kpi_level 'location_by_value', allocation 'value': pro rata to insured value; kpi_level 'location_by_loss', allocation 'loss': pro rata to loss; evaluate). Without them, a run of the individual scope takes each location's entity rows (kpi_level 'entity', allocation 'none': one location per design problem, so the payout is the location's own). A joint design (cluster or portfolio scope) without them has no per-location numbers (null): its entity rows are one of several optimal splits of the design problem's payout, the one the solver returned, and are never shown as per-location cover. runs gives the source of each run's numbers, allocations the rules in the bundle.",
    "allocations": [],
    "runs": [
      {
        "run_id": "sha256:74c5fbf1cf3d00f0cd6c57c5983b2b1baa6c592f732116d9816947f4ff743e11",
        "scope_key": "individual",
        "objective": "weighted_cvar",
        "kpi_level": "entity",
        "allocation": "none"
      }
    ]
  },
  "joint": {
    "rule": "Per-entity payout functions of a joint design are not unique. A payouts row has joint true when its design problem calibrates more than one payout entity jointly (the portfolio scope; a cluster scope calibrated jointly, with more than one cluster): such a problem is identified only through the sum over its entities, so the split of its payout across them is one of several optimal designs, the one the solver returned. A page that shows these rows per entity shows caption with them. runs lists the exported runs with such a problem. The individual scope and a cluster scope calibrated cluster by cluster (scope_key cluster_separate) have one entity per problem: their rows have joint false.",
    "caption": "Joint design: the payout split across entities is not unique. These per-entity payout functions are one of several optimal designs, chosen by the solver; read the portfolio level.",
    "runs": []
  }
